        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\begin{aligned}s_1&=a_1,\\\\s_2&=a_1s_1-2a_2,\\\\s_3&=a_1s_2-a_2s_1+3a_3,\\\\s_4&=a_1s_3-a_2s_2+a_3s_1-4a_4.\\end{aligned}$\n\nUse the quartic root-coefficient relationships for the first three sums; substitute all four roots into $Q(x)=0$ and add to find $s_4$.",
          "worked": "Let $E_1$ be the sum of the four roots, $E_2$ the sum of their six pairwise products, $E_3$ the sum of their four triple products and $E_4$ their product. The quartic root-coefficient relationships give $E_1=a_1$, $E_2=a_2$, $E_3=a_3$ and $E_4=a_4$.\n\nThe sum of roots and the expansion of its square give\n$$\\begin{align*}s_1&=E_1=a_1\\\\s_2&=E_1^2-2E_2\\\\s_2&=a_1s_1-2a_2.\\end{align*}$$\nExpanding $E_1s_2-E_2s_1$ cancels the terms containing two different roots and leaves $s_3-3E_3$. Thus,\n$$\\begin{align*}s_3&=E_1s_2-E_2s_1+3E_3\\\\s_3&=a_1s_2-a_2s_1+3a_3.\\end{align*}$$\nEach root satisfies the quartic:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\alpha^4-a_1\\alpha^3+a_2\\alpha^2-a_3\\alpha+a_4&=0\\\\\\beta^4-a_1\\beta^3+a_2\\beta^2-a_3\\beta+a_4&=0\\\\\\gamma^4-a_1\\gamma^3+a_2\\gamma^2-a_3\\gamma+a_4&=0\\\\\\delta^4-a_1\\delta^3+a_2\\delta^2-a_3\\delta+a_4&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nAdding gives $s_4-a_1s_3+a_2s_2-a_3s_1+4a_4=0$. Hence,\n$$\\begin{align*}s_4&=a_1s_3-a_2s_2+a_3s_1-4a_4.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 148
      }
    ]
